1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:06,220
I am thrilled to be up here with you today to open up this new series on parables of Jesus.

2
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:09,780
We're going to cover eight weeks of parables.

3
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:16,380
That means we're going to cover some that you probably have heard of and know very well by their current culture even,

4
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,980
and probably some that you don't know that well.

5
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:25,100
And along these eight weeks, we're going to be focused on how to understand the parables of Jesus

6
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,460
and how to respond to them appropriately, okay?

7
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:34,100
Which gives me the opportunity that I love to stand up on a church stage and say the following.

8
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:37,640
A pastor, a priest, and a rabbi walked into a barn.

9
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,820
You'd think one of them would have seen it.

10
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:49,920
Okay, so for those of you who laughed at that and only those who laughed, why is that funny, right?

11
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:52,660
I'm going to destroy a perfectly mediocre joke, okay?

12
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:54,760
Because it's important to understanding parables.

13
00:00:54,760 --> 00:01:01,260
Okay, so a joke is structured in such a way that I set up your expectations.

14
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:02,760
Two guys walk into a bar.

15
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:10,100
That means we all understand immediately, all right, it's some anonymous guy with fear of the virtues and all of the foibles of your average person, right?

16
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,900
And he's going to walk into a bar.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,120
This is a place with relaxed social standings and anything's possible.

18
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,020
A horse might talk to a knotted rope, right?

19
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:20,700
This is a joke structure.

20
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:22,740
Everybody's now ready for what's going to happen next.

21
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:25,660
And oh dear, it's a stupid grammar joke, right?

22
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:27,040
There's this twist at the end.

23
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:27,860
That's the punchline.

24
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:31,300
Where your expectations that were set up by the tropes are frustrated.

25
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:35,860
And in that moment of surprise delight, you giggle if you're polite to the speaker, right?

26
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:39,340
So how is that like a parable?

27
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:50,100
Well, in the narrative parables, Christ or the Old Testament prophet, whoever was telling the parable, would use the tropes and the basic assumptions of the time to set everybody's assumption in place.

28
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:52,160
Here's what, and they know what's going to happen next.

29
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:53,280
They understand this is a parable.

30
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:56,200
They know what's supposed to happen next and the teaching they're supposed to receive.

31
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:00,400
And then there's a twist and you frustrate those expectations.

32
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:07,020
And instead of giving you laughter, it gives you a moment to reconsider what you think is true and to change your behavior, right?

33
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:09,020
That's what a parable is for.

34
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:15,320
And therefore, to understand a parable, you have to understand their assumption.

35
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:20,000
You need to know the assumptions of the first century listener so you know what they were expecting to happen next.

36
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,660
So that you can identify that point of the twist, the punchline, right?

37
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:27,540
Because it's at that point that the meaning of the parable exists.

38
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:30,580
You can't just layer any old meaning on top of a parable.

39
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:32,680
Christ said it for a reason, right?

40
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,860
They are stories with specific intent.

41
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:39,020
Now, you may have heard growing up that parables are just stories Jesus told, and that's not wrong.

42
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:41,740
We tell stories because people remember them well.

43
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:52,220
Traditionally, rabbis, several rabbis have called parables a basket with handles because you can't really get all the truth out of a complex story right now.

44
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:58,840
But the story helps you carry it with you and dig around in the story later and get the truth out when you're ready to receive it.

45
00:02:58,840 --> 00:02:59,820
That's true.

46
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:03,000
But that doesn't really tell you how to get the truth out, right?

47
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:08,960
If you grew up in Baptist Sunday school, you heard that a parable is an earthly story with a...

48
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:09,820
Wow.

49
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:10,900
Yeah, exactly, right?

50
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:12,920
Everybody's heard that.

51
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:16,240
And it's not wrong, but how do you know the heavenly meaning, right?

52
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:18,280
What's the significance of the earthly story?

53
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:22,680
And a parable literally means parabolae.

54
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,720
It means something thrown alongside something else.

55
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:36,420
So it's a figurative story that parallels something that happens in real life or a concept that we're all familiar with that allows you to see in this figurative abstraction a new perspective on the thing that you've taken for granted, right?

56
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:38,260
And that's very true.

57
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:40,800
But it doesn't tell you how to understand it.

58
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:42,280
It just tells you what it is.

59
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:44,320
So we want to focus on how to understand it.

60
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:50,740
So in this series, we're going to cover some of the parables or narratives that really follow what I said.

61
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:52,800
Some of them are just sort of like evocative questions.

62
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,940
If this is true, how much more is that true?

63
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:55,680
And it makes you question.

64
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:56,100
I don't know.

65
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:57,240
How much more is that true, right?

66
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:01,480
It might be an ethical conundrum thrown out there to make you question your ethics.

67
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:06,840
The one we're going to talk about today is a series of small similitudes, just little word pictures.

68
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:08,540
And there is a punchline, okay?

69
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:11,880
There are many topics we're going to cover.

70
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:13,180
Jesus covered in the parables.

71
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:16,300
He talks about money, discipleship, prayer, the kingdom of heaven, the kingdom of Israel.

72
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:17,480
You have to know which is which.

73
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:23,480
And today, the parable we're going to talk about is about the purpose of parables.

74
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:35,240
And it's used in all the Gospels, well, three of the three, Matthew, Mark, and Luke, to sort of set up Jesus' teaching because it's key to understanding the rest of the parables that come after it, which is not surprisingly why we're talking about it first, right?

75
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:45,580
A few cautions that we need to keep in mind is that I'd like you to forget the titles of the parables that you know for the next eight weeks, okay?

76
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:47,960
Because the titles are fine.

77
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:51,460
We use shorthand to recognize what we're talking about.

78
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:56,140
But they're frequently baked into the title as an assumption about what the parable means.

79
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:59,240
The classic example is the parable of the unjust judge.

80
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:00,080
You may have heard of that.

81
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:04,320
A lady can't get what she needs from a judge and just keeps pestering.

82
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:08,700
And Christ lets us know that that's a parable about persistence in prayer.

83
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:09,840
It's got nothing to do with justice.

84
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:12,120
The judge is an incidental character, right?

85
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:16,180
So to call it the unjust judge predisposes you to misunderstand the parable.

86
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:20,660
Maybe we should call that one the parable of the persistent widow, right, instead of the unjust judge.

87
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:25,940
So let's forget the titles we know and forget the preconceptions that we might have.

88
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:33,300
Today's parable is actually a little bit unique in that Christ gave it a name.

89
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,020
The only parable Christ gave a name to.

90
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:36,420
He said this is the parable of the sower.

91
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:38,080
So we're good with this title.

92
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:47,660
And when we read a parable, we know there are layers of meaning in there.

93
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:49,000
There's lots to get out of it.

94
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,380
But that doesn't mean that any layer that you layer on top of it is valid.

95
00:05:53,040 --> 00:06:00,500
The church and Jewish scholars as well have a long history of turning parables into a...

96
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:06,860
They'll layer a lot of different allegorical tropes on top and align it with anything that they're currently concerned about.

97
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:11,320
What you have to do is focus on Christ's intent when he was telling the parable.

98
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:12,380
What's the twist?

99
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:16,340
It's not necessarily relevant to anything that you can make sound parallel to it, right?

100
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:21,140
You have to weigh it against first century context in Jesus' non-parabolic teachings

101
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:24,060
and the immediate context of the story that's around it, okay?

102
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:26,100
So that's what we're going to be doing for these eight weeks.

103
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:27,200
We're not getting...

104
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:32,720
And a really good rule of thumb, this isn't truism, but it's good practical advice, okay?

105
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:37,340
Is when you approach a parable, don't ask yourself, what does this mean?

106
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:41,580
What does it mean to turn into, what does it mean to me right now?

107
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:44,140
What does it mean in context of the politics I'm listening to on TV?

108
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:49,000
What does it mean, you know, in view of the trouble I'm having with my kids?

109
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:50,800
I know nothing about that.

110
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:58,680
The, it's what was it designed to do, that's the right question.

111
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:00,600
Why was Jesus telling this?

112
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:03,660
What is the response he was trying to elicit from his listeners, okay?

113
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:08,340
Because parables inform, but their primary purpose is to elicit a response.

114
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:13,040
We need to know the context, we need to see the point of the question, find that surprising impact,

115
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:16,660
and know then what response this could elicit from us, okay?

116
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:20,300
So that, with that in mind, that's what we're going to be doing over the next eight weeks.

117
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:24,760
And I'm just going to give you then the context of today's parable that seems important, okay?

118
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:28,020
So I'm going to read, it's in Matthew, Mark, and Luke.

119
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:31,640
I'm going to read it out of Mark because he's the most terse and it's easiest to misunderstand him.

120
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:38,340
So we want to make sure that we deal with all the difficult parts when we read it.

121
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:45,900
So, in Mark 1 through 3, Christ, it's a quick story of how Christ's notoriety has been building, right?

122
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:51,200
He starts in 127, he goes into his first synagogue to say something,

123
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:54,780
and then he frustrates religious leaders, and a couple of people come to hear what he has to say,

124
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:56,700
and he promotes miracles, and more crowds come to see him.

125
00:07:56,700 --> 00:08:03,340
And finally, in 321, his notoriety has grown to the point that crowds are coming to see him where he is.

126
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:05,180
He's not going out anymore, they're coming to see him.

127
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:10,000
And in 3.9, it tells us that he and his disciples have actually acquired a little bit of property.

128
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,560
They have a boat standing by, so that when the crowds get big, he could go out in this little inlet,

129
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:17,360
sit down, and speak to large crowds in sort of a virtual amphitheater, right?

130
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:21,340
And incidentally, in 1970, somebody went to where they believed this to have happened,

131
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:23,440
where, you know, where tradition says it happened,

132
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:26,000
and measured the number of people that could have heard him speaking,

133
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,280
and the maximum would have been around 7,000, okay?

134
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:32,400
So even if it was a half or a third of that, this is thousands of people coming, Luke tells us,

135
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:35,140
from many countries, to gather around this little inlet,

136
00:08:35,260 --> 00:08:38,020
he would push out his boat, the water would act as a sounding board,

137
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:39,300
and he would speak to the people.

138
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:46,120
And Mark records that Jesus then, after this path of growing notoriety,

139
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:48,080
who's come to this point where people come to hear him speak,

140
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:54,240
is now, in the beginning of chapter 4, seated in his boat, in the posture of a rabbi,

141
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:59,080
which is seated with people standing around you to hear in respect what you have to say,

142
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:03,160
and using the language and authority of an Old Testament prophet, these parables.

143
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:05,480
So he's really grown in his stature.

144
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:08,660
And Mark is deliberately setting up that picture for the people to see,

145
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:17,820
so they understand that he is a strong, well-regarded rabbi who has adopted the authority and the style of the prophet, okay?

146
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:21,020
So that's what the context is telling us.

147
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:26,820
And why are parables the language of the Old Testament prophet?

148
00:09:26,820 --> 00:09:28,800
That's the other context this then brings to us,

149
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:32,080
that the people who saw him beginning to use this language would have understood.

150
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:37,660
When an Old Testament prophet spoke, he was acting as the counterbalance to the authority of a king.

151
00:09:37,660 --> 00:09:41,260
And the kings don't like being told they're wrong, right?

152
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:45,660
They have armies at their disposal, and it's dangerous to tell a king they're wrong.

153
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:51,700
So the prophet had to find a way to deliver their statements of judgment.

154
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:53,320
You've done wrong, there's judgment coming.

155
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:55,820
Oh, and there's a path of impending deliverance,

156
00:09:55,820 --> 00:09:59,900
which inevitably includes the king humbling himself, which kings don't want to do, right?

157
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:03,040
So the classic example is David, right?

158
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:07,380
In 2 Samuel, David has taken another man's wife,

159
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:10,940
and he's had the man killed, and so that he can steal the wife,

160
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:14,240
and he's committed adultery, and Nathan comes to call him on the carpet.

161
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:16,640
But you don't just walk up to the king and yell at him.

162
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:18,500
You have to deliver it.

163
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:24,340
You have to veil your meaning only insofar as you can say indirectly what can't be said directly, right?

164
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:27,480
And that's what the parable served to do.

165
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:32,280
It's to move the important message past the listener's defenses, right, in the Old Testament.

166
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:39,700
So Nathan said, hey, there's a guy in your kingdom who's been stealing things from poor people and killing them.

167
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:42,260
And David says, off with his head, you know.

168
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:44,360
And Nathan says, punchline, here's the twist.

169
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:45,400
It's you, right?

170
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:50,440
And at that point, you know, David has to decide, is he going to respond to this parable?

171
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:51,380
Is he going to change his behavior?

172
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:54,320
And he does.

173
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:55,000
He's a great example.

174
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,600
Not of the first part, but of the repenting.

175
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:06,740
So Jesus doesn't have a king, he's speaking, but he's facing two types of hard opposition as he's starting to deliver these parables.

176
00:11:06,740 --> 00:11:08,780
The first one is a hardened heart.

177
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:12,060
That's Isaiah has talked about in Isaiah 6.

178
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:17,360
And we're going to spend a minute talking about Isaiah's prophecy that the hardened heart would have to be dealt with in just a minute.

179
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:21,320
So he has people that come that just don't want to hear what he has to say.

180
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:27,620
And there's a second group that are violently opposed, actively looking for people who might say unorthodox things.

181
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:30,440
And those are the people that eventually could kill Jesus, right?

182
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,320
Crucify him.

183
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:40,380
And Christ says in John that he spoke in parables in part because his time hadn't yet come and he didn't want those people to get enraged and crucify him yet, right?

184
00:11:40,380 --> 00:11:46,660
But most of the people he's talking to, he's having to get past their hard hearts, right?

185
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:48,360
Their defenses they put up.

186
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:50,660
Like a king of old that just doesn't want to hear it.

187
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:55,580
Now, in our context, the parables still deliver information to us today.

188
00:11:55,580 --> 00:12:06,380
And in our context, we cannot receive openly the things that we've limited ourselves not to hear, our preconceptions, right?

189
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:08,800
It could be a culturally imposed preconception.

190
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:10,900
It could be something we learned in church.

191
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:12,440
It could be a doctrine we've adopted.

192
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:19,160
But there's certain self-imposed limitations, whether culturally or personal, which just prevent us from hearing anything new.

193
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:22,500
And we sit grumpily and say, I don't want to receive that, right?

194
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:25,860
The parables can help get past those defenses, right?

195
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:32,460
Jesus taught with parables, like any good prophet, to appeal to his audience and to enable their hearing.

196
00:12:32,460 --> 00:12:36,300
When parables find a willing response, further explanation is given.

197
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:38,920
He'll always talk to somebody who asks for more information.

198
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:40,800
And he does that in today's parable, okay?

199
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:43,980
Where there's no response, the message is lost.

200
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:46,420
And Jesus explains that it'll happen in today's parable, okay?

201
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:51,880
That, incidentally, is a quote from Klein Snodgrass, who wrote an excellent book on parables.

202
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:57,140
If you've ever talked to me about parables, I've quoted him 20 times, even if I don't admit it, because I just love everything he says, okay?

203
00:12:57,140 --> 00:13:01,340
So I will not admit all the other things I take from him today, but there are lots of them.

204
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:12,400
So how clever would it be if instead of me getting up here and lecturing to you about parables, if I could tell you a figurative story to explain how they work and why they're important?

205
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:13,240
That'd be smart, right?

206
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:15,680
Well, Jesus did that, so I'm just going to quote him.

207
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:17,820
That's what he's done here.

208
00:13:17,820 --> 00:13:23,740
So we're going to read in Mark 4, 1 through 23, what Jesus had to say, okay?

209
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:25,260
So he's in this boat.

210
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:31,420
Verse 4, and again, he began to teach by the sea, and a very large crowd gathered about him.

211
00:13:31,420 --> 00:13:36,680
So he got into a boat and sat in it on the sea, and the whole crowd was beside the sea on the land.

212
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:38,900
And he was teaching them many things in parables.

213
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:48,380
And in his teaching, he said to them, listen, behold, a sower went out to sow, and as he sowed, some of the seed fell on the path where the birds came and devoured it.

214
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:55,160
And other seed fell on the rocky ground where it didn't have much soil, and immediately it sprang up since it had no depth of soil.

